A choice declares a nominal type whose values each carry one named variant.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    WindowEvent = {
        Opened,
        Resized | width I32, height I32 = 720 |,
        Closed,
    }
]

`Opened` and `Closed` are unit variants. `Resized` declares a payload with the
same typed field-list syntax as a struct. Its required `width` and defaulted
`height` both hold an `I32`.

### Declaration rules

- Write `Name = { ... }` with one or more comma-separated variants and an optional trailing comma.
- Use `PascalCase` for the choice and variant names. Each variant name must be unique within the choice.
- A unit variant carries no field list. A payload field list contains at least one field. Give each field a unique name, an explicit type and an optional default.
- A default must fully evaluate at compile time against the field's declared type. An optional type still requires a value unless the field declares a default.
- Payload fields are immutable. Inspect them with a pattern.
- A choice retains its own identity even when another choice has identical variants. A single-variant choice still uses its variant name.

`DoorEvent = { Opened }` introduces `DoorEvent::Opened`. A struct instead has
one constructor named after its type. See @../structs (Structs and methods) for field lists
and @../language-overview/#blocks-and-statements (Blocks and statements) for
declaration placement. Recursive choice declarations are deferred.

### Generic choices

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Maybe type A = {
        Some | value A |,
        Missing,
    }
]

Generic parameters follow the inference and instance rules in @../generics (Generics).
